TD  2 ------\ /------3 RD

         X

RD  3-------/ \------2 TD


GND 7----------------7 GND


Additionally, some null-modems tie pin 1 and pin 7 together (the two
grounds),

Also, pins 6,8,20 and 21, are often strapped together to present an
"always on" hardware condition. (These are often used by PC software
incorrectly to provide "hardware flow-control."


Manufacturers have long made special cables, one to connect a terminal
to a modem, and a different one to connect that same terminal directly
to a CPU.


One DB9 arrangement is as follows (the pins are usually numbered -
when you look in the shell with a strong light.)


         6  7  8  9 

       1  2  3  4  5


1-  AA  - FG    - Frame Ground, (earth ground) frequently jumpered to
signal ground

2-  BB  - RD   - Receive Data

3-  BA  - TD    - Transmit Data

4-  DTR - Data Terminal Ready

5-  GND - Signal Ground

6-  DSR - Data Set Ready

7-  RTS - Request to Send

8-  CTS - Clear to Send

9-  RI  - Ring Indicator


As you can see, this is different from the RS232 Standard which
defines the pins as follows. (Only the ones normally used for Async.)


Pin/Interchange / Common / description

    Circuit     / Name


 1- AA  - FG  - Frame Ground, (earth ground) frequently jumpered to
signal ground

 2- BA  - TD  - Transmit Data

 3- BB  - RD  - Receive Data

 4- CA  - RTS - Request to Send

 5- CB  - CTS - Clear to Send

 6- CC  - DSR - Data Set Ready

 7- AB  - SG  - Signal Ground

 8- CF  - CD  - Carrier Detect

20- CD  - DTR - Data Terminal Ready

21- CG  - SQ  - Signal Quality

22- CE  - RI  - Ring Indicator


Here's a couple of sites talking about RS232C.



http://mindprod.com/rs232c.html

http://users.breathemail.net/trevor_gowen/rs232/rs232.htm

http://www.airborn.com.au/rs232.html


Just Google for RS232C pin-outs... The info is also buried on the HP
website someplace, but I don't know where anymore.


</fontfamily>T.T.F.N.

