    male to female adapter in line to allow the modem cable to connect =
to the 9
    pin connector on the Alpha.

    Could this be the problem?


  Yes and no. It depends on if this "adapter" is simply a "Gender =
Bender" (converting mail to female) or a "Null Modem." A null-modem =
"flips" receive and transmit lines.


    I have also tried connecting another modem cable from a 9 pin (Com 1 =
on the
    WIN2K) to 25 pin (which is I think the modem port) on the Alpha, but =
there
    is no output.


  Historically, on Digital (DEC) equipment, you only need pins 2,3 and 7 =
-- receive data, transmit data and ground between a terminal and a CPU. =
The other lines are "handshake" lines normally only used by modems.

  So you need, what today is called a crossover cable, or historically, =
a null-modem.

  Serial communications are normally accomplished under RS232C -- which =
is a Standard, defined in terms of a 25 pin DB25 connector. It always =
describes the connections between a "modem" and a "terminal". The terms =
of the Standard are "DCE" - Data Communications Equipment, the modem - =
or "DTE" Data Terminal Equipment,=20
  the computer (which used to be a real VT100).

  IBM corrupted the standard when they introduced the PC and the 25 pin =
connector was abbreviated to a 9 pin, DB9 based connector. Today the =
9pin connector is commonly found, but it is not defined in the actual =
RS232C Standard.=20

  Since both Computers are "DTE" if you connect them together, the =
Transmitted Data is received by the Transmitted Data line, instead of =
the Received Data line. A null-modem flips pins 2 and three so that =
Transmitted Data is correctly received by Receive Data.

  A standard RS232C "null modem" configuration for DEC gear.

  TD 2 ------\ /------3 RD
  X
  RD 3-------/ \------2 TD

  GND 7----------------7 GND

  Additionally, some null-modems tie pin 1 and pin 7 together (the two =
grounds),
  Also, pins 6,8,20 and 21, are often strapped together to present an =
"always on" hardware condition. (These are often used by PC software =
incorrectly to provide "hardware flow-control."

  Manufacturers have long made special cables, one to connect a terminal =
to a modem, and a different one to connect that same terminal directly =
to a CPU.

  One DB9 arrangement is as follows (the pins are usually numbered - =
when you look in the shell with a strong light.)

  6 7 8 9=20
  1 2 3 4 5

  1- AA - FG - Frame Ground, (earth ground) frequently jumpered to =
signal ground
  2- BB - RD - Receive Data
  3- BA - TD - Transmit Data
  4- DTR - Data Terminal Ready
  5- GND - Signal Ground
  6- DSR - Data Set Ready
  7- RTS - Request to Send
  8- CTS - Clear to Send
  9- RI - Ring Indicator

  As you can see, this is different from the RS232 Standard which =
defines the pins as follows. (Only the ones normally used for Async.)

  Pin/Interchange / Common / description
  Circuit / Name

  1- AA - FG - Frame Ground, (earth ground) frequently jumpered to =
signal ground
  2- BA - TD - Transmit Data
  3- BB - RD - Receive Data
  4- CA - RTS - Request to Send
  5- CB - CTS - Clear to Send
  6- CC - DSR - Data Set Ready
  7- AB - SG - Signal Ground
  8- CF - CD - Carrier Detect
  20- CD - DTR - Data Terminal Ready
  21- CG - SQ - Signal Quality
  22- CE - RI - Ring Indicator

  Here's a couple of sites talking about RS232C.


  http://mindprod.com/rs232c.html
  http://users.breathemail.net/trevor_gowen/rs232/rs232.htm
  http://www.airborn.com.au/rs232.html

  Just Google for RS232C pin-outs... The info is also buried on the HP =
website someplace, but I don't know where anymore.

