(Sorry I'm replying so late; I've been single-mindedly immersed 
in fixing an XFS bug.)

On Friday 09 May 2003 04:47 am, Wouter Rademaker wrote:
> 2. Something with this lines in adriver.h :
>       #if LINUX_VERSION_CODE < KERNEL_VERSION(2, 5, 3)
>       #define need_resched() (current->need_resched)
>       #endif
>    Because of this error :
> /lib/modules/2.4.20/build/include/linux/sched.h:946:

Recent kernels (such as 2.4.20) define a need_resched() function 
in <linux/sched.h>.  Apparently, alsa-driver is creating a macro 
to compensate for kernel versions where this function supposedly 
does not exist or is not useful for its purposes.

alsa-driver apparently thinks kernel 2.5.3 is the earliest 
version with a useful need_resched() function.  It's pretty much 
right as far as 2.4.x goes--the need_resched() function in 
2.4.20 really doesn't do anything more than return 
current->need_resched.  The unlikely() macro makes it look like 
it does something more, but the unlikely() macro is currently 
just a straight drop-through.

Nevertheless, defining the need_resched() macro in the presence 
of a need_resched() function apparently confuses gcc/alpha 
(gcc/i386 doesn't complain).  Thus the problem you're having.

So commenting out the lines above would work for 2.4.20, although 
it would add the (minor) expense of yet another function call.  
It would likely break other kernel versions, though.  A more 
proper fix would be in the patch attached below, which 
apparently works for 0.9.2, 0.9.3, and 0.9.3a.

> 3. Something to solve this error:
>      memory_wrapper.c: In function
> `snd_compat_vmalloc_to_page': memory_wrapper.c:41: `mem_map'
> undeclared (first use in this function) memory_wrapper.c:41:
> (Each undeclared identifier is reported only once
> memory_wrapper.c:41: for each function it appears in.)
> memory_wrapper.c:28: warning: `page' might be used
> uninitialized in this function
>      make[1]: *** [memory_wrapper.o] Error 1
>      make: *** [compile] Error 1
>    Probably can't he find the declaration of mem_map, but I
> can't find it either.

Including <linux/sched.h> and <linux/mm.h> seems to solve it.

Sadly, since my LX164 has no soundcard, I can't actually test all 
this beyond compilation. :(

-- 
Kelledin
"If a server crashes in a server farm and no one pings it, does 
it still cost four figures to fix?"

--Boundary-00=_XwAy+hj2YfdTlwe
