Interestingly, Red Hat may be dead, but SuSE has managed to "bootleg" a 
new version for Alpha.

Begin forwarded message:
> From: Stefan Reinauer <stepan@suse.de>
> Date: Tue May 13, 2003  10:09:51  AM US/Eastern
> To: suse-axp@suse.com, axp-list@redhat.com
> Subject: [suse-axp] [announce] SuSE Linux 8.1 Alpha Edition available.
>
> Dear Alpha community,
>
> it's been quite some spare time nights that the old SuSE Alpha Team
> spent to present you a new version of SuSE Linux for Alpha machines.
>
> To safe more greasy words: It's available for download from
>       ftp://ftp.suse.com/pub/suse/axp/supplementary/8.1/
>
> There are quite a couple of reasons to update now, among them are:
>
>  * kernel 2.4.19
>  * gcc 3.2.2, glibc 2.2.5, binutils 2.12.90.0.15
>  * apache2, bind8/9, ..
>  * kde 3.0.4
>  * ...
>
> SuSE Linux 8.1 Alpha Edition has been tested on several EV56/EV6/EV67
> machines, but it might also work on newer/older Alpha systems.
>
> To install SuSE Linux 8.1 Alpha Edition on SRM systems, you need to
> download the installation iso, and burn it to a CD. Find the ISO at
> ftp://ftp.suse.com/pub/suse/axp/supplementary/8.1/boot/boot.iso
> Then you can start installation with "boot dkb0 -fl 0". Please make
> sure to insert your cdrom device instead of dkb0. For interactive
> startup you can use the "-fl i" instead of "-fl 0"
> Installation on Milo systems is a little bit more work. Find a short
> description and the milo images at
> ftp://ftp.suse.com/pub/suse/axp/supplementary/8.1/milo/
>
> Please note: SuSE Linux 8.1 Alpha Edition comes with absolutely no
> warranty and support whatsoever. We can not provide iso images of the
> complete distribution at the moment, but ftp installation works fine
> here and should be viable once the distribution arrived on all mirrors.
>
> Feel free to send bug reports, or hints to the SuSE AXP mailing list
> suse-axp@suse.com.
>
> Best regards,
>   Stefan Reinauer
>
> -- 
> Architecture Team
>     SuSE Linux AG
>
