On Friday, May 23, 2003, at 10:57 AM, Maurice Hilarius wrote:
> With regards to your message at 06:49 AM 5/23/03, Mike Foley. Where 
> you stated:
>>    So, just another case of Maurice jumping the gun
>>    and injecting a bit of drama into the remaining Alpha customers
>>    lives?
> A case of reporting what the official voices tell me, and by doing so 
> priming them to fix their rather convoluted methods of doing business, 
> Mike.
>
> All I tried to do is order a bunch of copies of Red Hat boxed sets, as 
> I have done many times before.

The basic problem HPQ has is STILL -- "Stealth Marketing!"

Those of us who were old Digital customers were used to the fact that 
DEC sales reps were really nothing but couriers who carried the 
detailed orders WE (the customers) put together (from the handbooks we 
had back in those days), back to an order processing facility. We knew 
they didn't know anything. But it didn't matter because the customer 
had the information available to them to make the correct decisions.

We hoped that the Marketing Prowess of Compaq would "solve" that 
problem... alas, the infection went the opposite direction. Q got rid 
of the Systems and Options Catalog and provided their VARs with less 
than useful (and frequently inaccurate) information.

Then we hoped that HP understands the concepts of "Sales and 
Marketing." But alas, we find that HPQ has adopted the old Digital 
Stealth Marketing ways....

Today, you talk to some phone bank in India (if you are lucky) who 
barely understands what you are talking about, and you have to believe 
(put up with) what they tell you. There is no mechanism to verify what 
they are telling you.

DECUS/ENCOMPASS, and now (I assume) the new HP-World will continue to 
bang upon their cages as we have done for years....

As the saying goes, sadly -- some things never change.

[By the way, if you are interested in visiting Georgia in August. 
Here's the Tru64 program - it's guaranteed to be about Alpha.

www.hpworld2003.com/scps/guides/interestDetail.jsp?interest_id=2106

If you follow the link back to the Home page, you can visit the Linux 
track, but from what I can tell, it's all x86.]

T.T.F.N.
