Having spent a couple of days relocating my home office into a nicer larger room with a view I decided it was time to upgrade the Multia I use as my Internet gateway/firewall/wireless access point.

As this system has a IDE laptop hard drive I have to use MILO to boot the system. Following various previous discussions, I updated Milo to the latest version I could find (2.2.18-noname) and attempted to boot from the install CD that I downloaded a month or so ago. After fixing up the boot command to reflect reality (boot scd0:/kernels/vmlinux.gz initrd=/images/cdrom.img) the Multia starts to boot, appears to get a reasonable way in, then dies complaining that it can't find a root file system.

After watching the boot several times, I noticed the error message "initrd is too large - would overwrite kernel - aborting" going by. A quick web search gave the following informative message http://lists.debian.org/debian-alpha/2002/debian-alpha-200204/msg00157.html that describes the problem and a potential solution. Has this issue been solved for RedHat users? If not, am I doomed to run 6.2 until I upgrade my hardware?

Any help appreciated.


Huw Davies
BUSINESS CRITICAL SERVICES
Compaq - a Hewlett-Packard Company


E-mail: Huw.Davies@hp.com

* Personal opinion only *

 




