Perhaps somewhat OT, but .. you can run Open Office on an Alpha machine .. 
you just need Tru64 to do it.  A T64 port was finished early this year and is 
available in binary at 

http://h30097.www3.hp.com/affinity/portability.html

along with a lot of other open-sourcve stuff, some of which works better than 
others.

Myself I kind of like the AbiWord that came with the 7.2 distro.  It works 
well and will open up my whole hard drive full of WordPerfect files.  I 
haven't used the KOffice spreadsheet much, but it seems to me a "work in 
progress"

On the larger subject of the demise of Alpha, it seems to me that it has 
never been a better time to get into this platform.  The EV 56 machines are 
plentiful and cheap, and the EV6x generation is just starting to come 
off-lease.  

There probably won't be much for new Linux distributions, just as the latest 
version of Tru64 will probably be the last.  But perhaps some incremental 
improvements to the 7.2 will take place?  Perhaps something will happen 
similar to the way game kids are hacking new drivers for their Voodoo 5 cards.

Meanwhile, a lot of Linux coders learned their 64-bit chops on Alpha.  If you 
want to buy an Itanium or Opteron today and run a desktop OS, you've got 
exactly one choice (but lots of versions of that choice.)  That choice is 
*not* Windows.  

I use the Alpha platform to run a GIS program on Tru64.  I don't need support 
or a new distro of anything.  The program runs great as-is, much better than 
the equipment I learned on.  I may buy an extra machine for spares ...

Meanwhile I for one really appreciate the work that George and Mike and a few 
others are doing.  perhaps a good thing the "user communtiy" could help with 
is establishing a strategic direction and priorities list for the 7.2; 
perhaps the SuSe and the RedHat hackers should get together on it?

Just a few stray thoughts ..

jn



