On Sun, 2003-05-25 at 10:55, Uncle George wrote:
> Running around accusing intel, dec, olson just wont help. Thats history.

I don't think I was "blaming" anyone.  In fact, I totally agree it is
all hindsight now.

Intel's IA-64 EPIC/Predication approach was very innovative -- heck,
extremely innovative.  But sometimes engineering ideals don't turn out
well in real silicon.

> I have an alpha. I have the Openoffice Source, and I'm going to try to 
> port the product to alpha, despite intel, m$, compaq,hp, marketing, or 
> other architectures that have a better future.

I originally dislike the concept of the AMD x86-64 ISA, as did most
people.  But they have combined a lot of excellent, system-level ideas
(e.g., HyperTransport) into the whole package at the same time.  Despite
many people who said otherwise, the NexGen RISC86 approach is still
scaling in the Opteron and Athlon 64 -- far longer than I even
anticipated.  Maybe AMD was right all along.

Intel hasn't designed a new 32-bit processor core since 1993, with their
Pentium 4 being designed quickly, with long pipelined stages and not a
lot of efficiency.  With IA-64 not coming to the desktop until later in
the decade, we have little other options besides sticking with IA-32 for
now.  So AMD did the best it could and it shocked me.  But then again,
AMD sports a number of former Digital engineers and API Networks is
still making an impact as well.

> BTW its unfortunate that you harp on the fx!32 m$ simulator. Its an 
> interesting product, ate up lots of core, cpu time, disk space, just so 
> one could run an i386 ( i suspect an m$ ) product.

I only bring up FX!32 because Intel has now!

Why is it wrong to point out that FX!32 could have let us move away from
x86 long ago?  I'm not talking about using FX!32 as a permanent
solution, just a temporary solution while we move people to a new
architecture.  That should have happened long ago.  Digital proved that
it is very easy to emulated (and "binary recompile") compatibility for
the same OS, but different platform.

Again, the sad thing is that Intel is now trying to do the _same_thing_
they lambasted Digital for with the Alpha.  Here's Intel, who at one
point said they didn't like the Alpha + FX!32 approach and wanted to
build an x86 compatible processor in the first place, using the same
technology!  What irony is it to see that they need FX!32 now for their
own product!

> It did really well with photoshop.  Too bad dec/compaq couldnt convince
> those entites to do a native port.

Correct.  Because Alpha was not Intel's focus.  Intel's focus is the
key.  It's the reason why we have the USB 2.0 bastardization instead of
FireWire.  It's interesting to even hear Intel engineers admit that not
supporting FireWire in its southbridge and, worse yet, the creation of
USB 2.0 is one of the worst things they have done.

> Even the m$ NT/alpha system was built as a 32bit system.

>From my understanding, the 64-bit NT/Alpha port had existed at Digital
as soon as 1996.  But the Alpha wasn't a commodity part from Intel, so
it never surfaced.  But Microsoft is sure reaping the benefits of that
first port now in its 64-bit Windows versions.

But FX!32 technology also made its way into OpenVMS and Linux as well.

> BTW#2 exactly who would buy a +$4000 21264 just so u can run the 
> equivalent of a $300 pII.

Had the Alpha been a commodity Intel processor, it would have been lower
costing (economies of scale).  With commodity, you get a commodity
platform.  We would have had 64-bit Windows long ago, with more native
ports.  And for the few ports that didn't make it, FX!32 would have
filled the bill nicely.

Instead we're 3-4 years later with Intel's 64-bit attempt.  One that
really less commodity than Alpha was at its hey-day, and even has to use
FX!32 to run a number of apps.  Again, the irony!



-- 
