On Sun, 2003-05-25 at 10:31, Maurice Hilarius wrote:
> Right. But they fell into a market trap.

It's hard to fight economies of scale.  But Intel could have changed all
that by sticking with Digital in the early '90s and slowly adopting
Alpha in the latter '90s.

Instead we got an Intel who fought hard, successfully, to keep Microsoft
from releasing a 64-bit release of Windows for Alpha, and promising the
world with their IA-64 design.  A design that will not be commodity
until almost a decade after the Alpha could have become.

> And so did Intel with Itanium.

But unlike Itanium, the Alpha built on proven RISC technologies.

The Alpha took them to the anal power by removing all 8 and 16-bit
instructions (although 8 and 16-bit loads and stores were added in the
21164+).  When they added new A/V instruction they only added 5, because
the Alpha design was based on never holding up the pipeline due to an
instruction that required too many cycles.  Again, totally anal.  But as
Digital successfully proved with FX!32, binary emulation and eventual,
run-time binary profiling and recompilation was the best way to maintain
binary compatibility on the same OS with a new architecture.

Intel, on the other hand, bet on EPIC to keep the pipelines full and
Predication to remove the need for branch prediction.  While I and
everyone thought the EPIC/Predication concepts were extremely
innovative, sometimes even the greatest innovation does not mean
real-world performance.

The pre-Itanium sample quotes I read from Alpha design engineers
predicted that the Itanium would fail to keep the pipelines filled any
more than RISC, because it relied totally on compile-time optimization
whereas Alpha had found that run-time optimization isd still very much
required.  They also predicted that Predication was far too wasteful and
would only be good when used selectively with a combination of run-time
branch prediction, and not totally like the original Itanium design.

Sure enough, the Alpha design team was correct.  The original Itanium
(Merced) with 100% compile-time EPIC optimiation and 100% Branch
Predication flopped.  Even the Itanium 2 (McKinley) is still trying to
"work back in" run-time optimization and branch prediction and still
facing serious run-time performance issues in the architecture with
keeping the pipelines full and not over-wasting cycles on the branch not
taken in predication.

As I previously stated, Intel ended up adopting FX!32 anyway.  Unlike
Alpha, the IA-64 has chip space dedicated x86 microcode (as well as some
HP-PARC compatibility) -- all of which is not well used.  So I won't be
surprised if the x86 microcode, other than maybe a few dedicated
routines and circuits for SSE/vector, is removed in the Itanium 4.

Furthermore, Intel had originally predicted that its Itanium 3
(Deerfield and Madison) would solve the major of the issues, and see its
first desktop processor release in 2004/2005.  But even Intel has now
stated that they don't see a 64-bit desktop until near the end of the
decade, which probably means they don't see the Itanium 3 offering a
desktop role either.

So we're back to Itanium going commodity a full decade after the Alpha
could have.  Yes, hindsight.  But still the reality.

> It does not matter how good it is.  If there is no software for it.

The problem with Itanium (especially the original) is that it does not
perform well even with its own native code!  I'm not even talking x86
compatibility at this point.

> 32 bit X86 compatibility is the key.

Again, even taking x86 compatibility out of the equation, the original
Itanium was a disaster.  The Itanium 2 is still a horrendously
performing desktop chip for the transistor cost.  I mean, even if it had
the economies of scale that the Pentium 4 did, it would still be much
larger and much more costly to produce for little advantage other than a
few FPU marks.

But there Intel will only be a victim of its own success.  It has moved
the entire non-engineering/scientific software industry to using its
lossy SSE/vector instructions to avoid having to use the Pentium 4's
sub-standard FPU.  So the Itanium doesn't offer much there for
non-engineering/scientific applications either -- even if it was just as
cheap.


-- 
