And why would Intel back DECs CPU??
That would/could/did not happen.
Instead they maneuvered with Compaq and HP and assimilated/killed it.
And, as you said, fell into the same trap as DEC, building a 64 bit only 
CPU, with no X86 compatibility, at a frighteningly high cost.

>Instead we got an Intel who fought hard, successfully, to keep Microsoft
>from releasing a 64-bit release of Windows for Alpha, and promising the
>world with their IA-64 design.  A design that will not be commodity
>until almost a decade after the Alpha could have become.

Good thing for us that Microsoft has grown tired of waiting and have 
committed to 64 bit Windows for X86-64 already.
For Itanium what I hear is the M$ is only doing a server edition, at least 
for now, further re-inforcing the cost performance advantage of X86-64


>Furthermore, Intel had originally predicted that its Itanium 3
>(Deerfield and Madison) would solve the major of the issues, and see its
>first desktop processor release in 2004/2005.  But even Intel has now
>stated that they don't see a 64-bit desktop until near the end of the
>decade, which probably means they don't see the Itanium 3 offering a
>desktop role either.
There is now ay that Intel is going to release Itanium technology for the 
desktop soon, as long as they can milk P3 ( and it's offshoot Centrino), 
P4, XEON P4 for at least 2 more years.
OTOH, AMD, which is not as deeply invested in 32 bit can now happily move 
over completely to X86-64 this year.

About the only thing stopping them from taking a pronounced lead in the 
next two years is market spin and production capacity.




