On Sun, May 25, 2003 at 06:17:14PM +1000, Davies, Huw wrote:
> 
> I fear that rebuilding the kernel is the solution. Of course, as this
> is currently my only Linux/alpha box it's a little hard to build a 
> suitable kernel until I can get it installed....

Why?  Is your current box a compiler deficient?  Of course with your own
kernel you need to boot from floppies with that kernel and a
corresponding ramdisk or make yourself a custom boot CD.  Milo, once it
is loaded from somewhere, does not require anything "special" on boot
media.

First boot may also have some "interesting moments" but you can use the
same custom kernel you built instead of a default one.  Your kernel can
be loaded from wherever you can find it; it does not have to a be a root
(or /boot) partition.  When you booted once a new installation then you
can make all of this more convenient.

