On Sun, 2003-05-25 at 12:21, Maurice Hilarius wrote:
> And why would Intel back DECs CPU??  That would/could/did not happen.

Don't you know your history?

In the late-'80s and early '90s, many firms came to together to consider
standardizing on the bi-endian MIPS RISC architecture.  Naturally, the
consortium never came to bear fruit as Sun had its own Berkeley SPARC
architecture, and many other vendors thought they could do better.

But many partnerships formed.  IBM and Motorola went off to create a new
Power-series ISA that eventually became the PowerPC.  Lesser known was
the fact that Intel began to collaborate with Digital on their new
64-bit develpoment, which would become Alpha.

Intel would then break the relationship before Alpha was released.  But
Intel brought many of its design concepts to the Pentium Pro.  Not
surprisingly a lawsuit ensued years later, which led to Intel's eventual
purchase of Digital's fabs in the settlement.  And everything else is
history.

> Instead they maneuvered with Compaq and HP and assimilated/killed it.

I'm talking about events that happened much earlier.  Again, it's all
hindsight now.

> And, as you said, fell into the same trap as DEC, building a 64 bit only 
> CPU, with no X86 compatibility, at a frighteningly high cost.

Actually, the Alpha 21164 became quite a good, commodity design whereas
Itanium has yet to even have anything equivalent even some 6 years
later.

> Good thing for us that Microsoft has grown tired of waiting and have 
> committed to 64 bit Windows for X86-64 already.
> For Itanium what I hear is the M$ is only doing a server edition, at least 
> for now, further re-inforcing the cost performance advantage of X86-64

We could have had the same with the Alpha had Intel realized it was a
much better and, more importantly, quickier avenue to 64-bit.

> There is now ay that Intel is going to release Itanium technology for the 
> desktop soon, as long as they can milk P3 ( and it's offshoot Centrino), 
> P4, XEON P4 for at least 2 more years.

The problem is that P3/P4/Xeon's don't natively support more than 4GB of
RAM.  They use an extension that is basically the same thing EMS was in
DOS, a poor-performing hack.

Many applications, especially development, need more than 4GB of RAM,
and many end-users will approach that "limit" very soon.

> OTOH, AMD, which is not as deeply invested in 32 bit can now happily move 
> over completely to X86-64 this year.

AMD is finally going to do something Intel has never done with IA-32,
"standardize" it.  In the P4, Intel has even reused 386 opcodes for new
SSE instructions, rendering the P4 as the least 386 compatible processor
yet.

> About the only thing stopping them from taking a pronounced lead in the 
> next two years is market spin and production capacity.

And the fact that they lag Intel 9+ months in fabrication technology. 
Hopefully their partnership with IBM will change that in short order.


