On Sun, 2003-05-25 at 20:00, rm.riches@verizon.net wrote:
> Being as it contains the same old dirty, ugly, inefficient
> X86 instruction set (prefix bytes, segment registers, A20M
> pin, etc.) and the 64-bit instruction set is based on the
> old X86 instruction set, IMHO, it does not meet the "clean
> and efficient" criterion.

Actually, if I'm reading the AMD volumes correctly, segmentation is
_removed_ in x86-64 "Long" mode.  There are other well thought out
design implementations as well with the x86-64 ISA in general.

As such, AFAIK, AMD is making x86 _better_ with x86-64.  If anything, at
least they are not just adding more useless SIMD instructions for the
sake of breaking compatibility *COUGH*Intel*COUGH*.

Case-in-point:  AMD is about simplifying IA-32 with its x86-64 ISA --
and _not_ continually making it more and more idiosyncratical like Intel
has over the last few years. 


