On Sun, 2003-05-25 at 21:39, Mike A. Harris wrote:
> It is a very good performer however, and it is very likely to 
> succeed IMHO because it will commoditize quickly due to it's 
> greatest weakness also being it's greatest strength.  That is x86 
> compatibility.

It's all about economies of scale.  Assuming AMD can produce the x86-64
in significant quantity later this year with the release of the Athlon
64 (uP x86-64), and 2nd generation Opteron (MP x86-64), then it doesn't
matter what design or ISA is best.  Which I still think people are
missing the point on.

Intel is not deliverying a commodity 64-bit product _regardless_ of
ISA.  That is, _even_ if you remove Windows and its x86 compatibility
issue from the equation, IA-64 just does not offer a commodity solution
at this time.  It's too big, too expensive and too immature IMHO.  Yes,
IA-64 will _eventually_ get there, I mean it _is_ innovative, but Intel
basically had to "start from scratch" after the original Itanium design
flopped and _disproved_ all their theories about how well EPIC and
Predication would work.

So now we're back to the question of _today_ -- how do I get good
performance on a system with more than 4GB of RAM?  This is what not
only "high-end" engineering/scientific workstation users are asking, but
even your typical software developer who needs 4GB+ now.  So developers
have four choices:  RISC (expensive), IA-64 (expensive), P4/Xeon (slow)
or Opteron/Athlon64 (inexpensive and fast).  Bam!  It's a no-brainer. 
Especially if you consider the fact that the Opteron/Athlon64 still
offers x86 compatibility!

> Any processor coming to market that can't run x86 code either in
> hardware, or with some form of hardware and/or software emulation
> and run it at equivalent or better speed of competing x86 chips,
> is unlikely to commoditize on the massive market out there where
> the volume is.

Again, I think it's more than just the x86 compatibility game.  Yes,
that's a great "bonus" -- one that matters on Windows.  But for Linux,
it's not quite as important.  But when my only other options are other,
expensive 64-bit platforms (again, RISC or IA-64) or a 32-bit platform
with performance-reducing "hacks" (again, P4/Xeon), AMD Opteron/Athlon64
becomes the _obvious_choice_.  x86 compatibility is, again, just a
"bonus."

> AMD64 does x86 natively in hardware, and generally faster than
> other comparable x86 chips.  Another way of looking at AMD64, is 
> that it is the next generation 32bit x86 CPU which has 64bit 
> thrown in for free.

With that all said, what is Red Hat's x86-64 plans?  Will we have an AMD
x86-64 dedicated binary distribution?  If so, will it be just
Enterprise, Consumer ("regular" Linux), both?  Or maybe just a few
x86-64 build "core" packages like the kernel, GLibC, GTK+/Gnome, etc...?

Inquiring users want to know -- like me -- currently writing this on a
Red Hat Linux 9 system powered by a dual-Athlon MP2400+ with 1GB SDRAM.


-- 
