On Sun, 2003-05-25 at 22:29, Mike A. Harris wrote:
> I agree, the AMD64 technology extends x86 to 64bit quite nicely 
> IMHO, and when running in 64bit mode, a lot of the x86 cruft/crap 
> is removed, which makes it a somewhat cleaner architecture.

We can play the hindsight game repeatedly.

First off, everything probably would have been better if everyone just
adopted the bi-endian MIPS architecture at the turn of the last decade. 
But it didn't happen.

Secondly, even the best strategies always have bumps and miscues.  E.g.,
IBM ended up leaving Motorola on 32-bit PowerPC compatibility when its
first 64-bit PowerPC design, the PPC620, failed, and IBM moved to a
64-bit Power-only ISA.  Even though IBM is now introducing its first
64-bit Power4 chip with 32-bit PowerPC compatibility, the PPC970, they
are still also sampling a new Power5 design that's a 64-bit Power-only
ISA.

As such, even if Intel had stuck with Digital on the Alpha, it may have
splintered as well into an EPIC and Predication approach anyway.

Third, the last full IA-32 processor design Intel did was the Pentium
Pro in 1993.  Intel had assumed that 64-bit would be here by now, and
it's IA-64 would be king.  Well, Intel was half-right at least ;-p.

But (ignoring the vector/SSE marketing gimmicks and MHz game) you have
to give them kudos on getting something like the Pentium 4 out in only
18-months -- instead of the typical 3-5 years for a processor.  This is
a feat only matched in the x86 world by IDT's WinChip release, which was
done in very much the same manner (simplified but scalable pipes, major
performance hits in stalls, etc...).

>From my understanding, even the original NexGen RISC86 core, of which
all modern AMD processors owe their heritage to, took almost 8 years to
design.



