On 25 May 2003, Bryan J. Smith wrote:

>> It is a very good performer however, and it is very likely to 
>> succeed IMHO because it will commoditize quickly due to it's 
>> greatest weakness also being it's greatest strength.  That is x86 
>> compatibility.
>
>It's all about economies of scale.  Assuming AMD can produce the x86-64
>in significant quantity later this year with the release of the Athlon
>64 (uP x86-64), and 2nd generation Opteron (MP x86-64), then it doesn't
>matter what design or ISA is best.  Which I still think people are
>missing the point on.

I agree completely.  As long as AMD can commoditize the chip, 
which presumes they will be able to produce it in the necessary 
quantities, I think it will be quite successful.

>Intel is not deliverying a commodity 64-bit product _regardless_
>of ISA.  That is, _even_ if you remove Windows and its x86
>compatibility issue from the equation, IA-64 just does not offer
>a commodity solution at this time.  It's too big, too expensive
>and too immature IMHO.  Yes, IA-64 will _eventually_ get there,
>I mean it _is_ innovative, but Intel basically had to "start
>from scratch" after the original Itanium design flopped and
>_disproved_ all their theories about how well EPIC and
>Predication would work.

On a personal opinion note, I basically agree with Linus's
viewpoint on ia64.


>So now we're back to the question of _today_ -- how do I get
>good performance on a system with more than 4GB of RAM?  This is
>what not only "high-end" engineering/scientific workstation
>users are asking, but even your typical software developer who
>needs 4GB+ now.  So developers have four choices:  RISC
>(expensive), IA-64 (expensive), P4/Xeon (slow) or
>Opteron/Athlon64 (inexpensive and fast).  Bam!  It's a
>no-brainer.  Especially if you consider the fact that the
>Opteron/Athlon64 still offers x86 compatibility!

Bingo.


>> Any processor coming to market that can't run x86 code either in
>> hardware, or with some form of hardware and/or software emulation
>> and run it at equivalent or better speed of competing x86 chips,
>> is unlikely to commoditize on the massive market out there where
>> the volume is.
>
>Again, I think it's more than just the x86 compatibility game.  
>Yes, that's a great "bonus" -- one that matters on Windows.  
>But for Linux, it's not quite as important.  But when my only
>other options are other, expensive 64-bit platforms (again, RISC
>or IA-64) or a 32-bit platform with performance-reducing "hacks"
>(again, P4/Xeon), AMD Opteron/Athlon64 becomes the
>_obvious_choice_.  x86 compatibility is, again, just a "bonus."

You're looking at things purely from a Linux perspective though, 
and Linux isn't the majority of what sells computers *yet*.  What 
I'm saying, is that in order for AMD64 to be truely successful, 
it has to be stocked and sold daily in every computer store 
around the country, for Grandma's home PC, etc.  Mass market 
commoditization.  This means Windows.  In effect, what is 
likely to make AMD64 a successful 64bit CPU is it's 32bit 
x86 compatibility - Windows running faster on AMD64 in 32bit 
mode as "the next generation Athlon" is what will sell the chip 
and make it cheap for people to purchase.  The 64bit aspect is 
thrown in for free so to speak.  As long as the commoditization 
does kick in, which I believe it will, then those wanting or 
needing 64bit computing will end up getting it for free 
basically.


>> AMD64 does x86 natively in hardware, and generally faster than
