>From what I understand, the core of the Pentium 4 was designed in 18
months -- less than half the time of basically every single other major
x86 core design Intel (among just about any other x86 designer) has
attempted.  This was an amazing feat, at an expected cost.

There might have been some vector/SIMD changes that pushed the release
back some -- but the short timing of the release was of the essence.  At
least this is my understanding.

> There was very little, other than basic microarchitectural concepts
> of a "dynamic execution" processor, taken from the Pentium Pro and
