design, with exception of IDT's WinChip and, as discussed here, Intel's
Pentium 4 which only took 18 months.  Add in additional features (like
vector/SIMD), peripherials and components and, most importantly, timing
closure, and I'm sure the entire Pentium 4 design cycle was 36 months.

Now if you have information that says the core of the Pentium 4 took no
longer than any other major Intel x86 design (the Pentium, Pentium Pro,
etc...), then I'll believe you.  But it was to my understanding that the
Pentium 4 core was designed in less than half the time any previous,
major Intel x86 core.

