I want to apologize for extending the off-topic thread into Intel and
AMD far longer than most of you probably cared to hear it here.  It was
clearly off-topic.

I also want to thank Mr. Harris for letting me know a list dedicated to
the x86-64 existed.  That will keep me from posting anything other than
Alpha for awhile on this list, as I did not know it existed before
today.  ;-ppp

Lastly, I am interested in hearing more from Mr. Riches about his
knowledge of the Pentium 4's design.  Thus far my knowledge comes
largely from one former Intel engineer who worked on the Pentium 4. 
Since my semiconductor design experience is rather limited (despite my
educational background, far too much of my career has been in technology
or software engineering and not much in semiconductor design), and well
outside the x86 arena altogether (basically only 8 and 32-bit Motorola
microcontrollers).  So I'd rather know the story he saw from his far
more in-depth venue.


