BWH> And so, you've all become customers of the Debian AXP release...

No way:)


I don't want to start a 'which linux is better' flame
so I'll tell about only the facts why not.

Debian's market share has dropped below 3% (for comparsion
Redhat and SuSE owns 23% and 18%), and it still has
that ugly, unfriendly management programs and needs
lot of manual work.

Today's key word is >efficiency< and >inefficiency<
whatever you do in a corporate enviroment must be
efficient like linux out of the box offers solution
(what Redhat and SuSE offers).

The old, inefficiency way (need-to-recompile-
X-packages-apply-Y-patches-and-buld-almost-the-whole-
thing-from-scratch-will-only-take-two-and-a-half-day)
is simply not working.

I'm not against debian, because it has lot of nonprofit
work in it and there are many very good
distributions based on it, eg. Knoppix (simply:great!)
or Gibraltar (sigle CD bootable firewall distrib with full
webadmin, the fw rules stored on write protected
floppy), but it seems to me that Deb has more and more
handycaps by the time goes on.



Regards, Zsolt



