On 26 May 2003, Bryan J. Smith wrote:

>On Sun, 2003-05-25 at 23:01, Mike A. Harris wrote:
>> On a personal opinion note, I basically agree with Linus's
>> viewpoint on ia64.
>
>I don't entirely.  Not to knock Linus, but he's looking at IA-64 from a
>CS/ISA/programmer view.  He's not looking at it from a
>gate/semiconductor/layout (let alone timing) design view.  It's an
>entirely different world.

I disagree.  Linus is looking at it from a perspective of what
processors are bound to be successful in volume based on the fact
that some kind of x86 binary execution support with excellent
performance is required in order to succeed in massive volume 
sales.  You can design the best 32/64/128bit architecture since 
sliced bread, make it totally legacy free with zero or close to 
zero flaws - a computer of the Gods so to speak, and if it 
doesn't have x86 execution capability with x86-like or better 
performance, it wont ever be anything but a niche CPU used in 
enterprise markets, and sell nowhere near the volume of the mass 
market of x86 CPUs.

Granted, this depends on the ability of a manufacturer to be able
to produce CPUs to meet demands, but that is a different
discussion.  Assuming the capability to produce infinite CPUs,
x86 compatibility is what will ultimately and undeniably
determine if any CPU will ever reach the deployment size that x86
currently holds.


>I'm still amazed AMD has been able to pull it off.  Not only
>from an innovation standpoint, but the fact that they are taking
>someone _elses_ mess and improving it better than the original
>could.

AMD has always been a good innovator IMHO, so I'm not too
surprised personally.  I've had various AMD processors in the
past, from AMD 386DX40 to various AMD 486 models, K5, K6, K6-2,
K6-3.  I'd have several Athlon systems as well, if I didn't have
so many Pentium family loaner systems. I can't justify buying 
new hardware until these ones are too slow.  ;o)


>> You're looking at things purely from a Linux perspective though, 
>> and Linux isn't the majority of what sells computers *yet*.  What 
>> I'm saying, is that in order for AMD64 to be truely successful, 
>> it has to be stocked and sold daily in every computer store 
>> around the country, for Grandma's home PC, etc.  Mass market 
>> commoditization.  This means Windows.
>
>Correct and I totally agree.
>
>My removal of Windows/x86-compatibility from the "equation" in previous
>arguments was just to show that AMD is _still_ a far better option for
>software developers who just need a high performing platform with more
>than 4GB RAM support without spending a fortune.  AMD is it and it only.

Absolutely.  My real point however wasn't to show what 
processor(s) are decent for doing certain computing tasks, or 
which processor(s) are good for Linux developers, enthusiasts, 
etc.  My point was to state that x86 legacy compatibility is the 
absolute number one critical feature that any CPU requires in 
order to be mass market commoditized.  That has nothing to do 
with Linux really, it has to do with what processors the mass 
market out there uses.  Right now that is x86, and the majority 
of them are running Windows.  In order to get another CPU out 
there that is just as popular as x86, it will have to do what x86 
does now, and do it better.  The mass market isn't ready to drop 
all of their existing binary applications and buy 
Alpha/PPC/MIPS/SPARC/whatever applications.  Nobody on the 
desktop (which is where the volume is) will purchase a CPU unless 
they can run their x86 software on it.

All other CPUs are for special and niche markets and will never 
hit x86 volume levels.  AMD64 however is all that *and* a bucket 
of chicken.

This doesn't counter your statements at all however as you are 
making one point which is not related to the point which I'm 
making, and both are IMHO correct.

>> In effect, what is likely to make AMD64 a successful 64bit CPU is
>> it's 32bit x86 compatibility - Windows running faster on AMD64 in
>> 32bit mode as "the next generation Athlon" is what will sell the chip 
>> and make it cheap for people to purchase.  The 64bit aspect is 
>> thrown in for free so to speak.
>
>Yes, from the vantage of Windows.

No, it has nothing to do with Windows vs. Linux vantage point.  
I am not talking about what CPU is best for what OS, or for the
users of that OS.

I am talking about the more CPUs that go out the door, the
cheaper those CPUs are, and the easier they are to get.  What
currently sells the most number of CPUs, is what CPUs run 
Microsoft Windows.  Any CPU that does not, will never reach the 
volume of distribution that x86 does, and will thus be more 
expensive because it doesn't sell in high volume.

If someone is running Linux, and don't care what architecture you
use as long as it does what you need, and don't care if you pay
through the nose for a niche CPU, then the above probably doesn't
matter.

Again, two different unrelated discussions however.


