On 26 May 2003, Bryan J. Smith wrote:

>> And add the fact that it executes 32 bit code AND 64 bit code at
>> the same time.  It is not a "switch", but, in effect, two processors.
>
><anal=on>
>Er, that statement seems to imply a technical reality that is not so.  
></anal>
>
>That's like saying the Intel 386 was also two processors because it
>could run both 386 Protected mode software as well as 8086/8088 software
>in Virtual 386 mode at the same time.

It depends on if you're looking at it from a physical or logical 
viewpoint.  If one requires x86 compatibility for some 
applications and also requires 64bit arch for other applications, 
and prefers they both run on one machine, then AMD64 is 
/logically/ two processors in one compared to many other possible 
solutions.  The alternative is purchasing an x86 box, and an 
alternative 64bit processor such as Alpha.

It's obviously not 2 physical processors, but logically/virtually
it is.


-- 
