On Mon, 26 May 2003, Stefan Reinauer wrote:
> PPC and Power4 cpus don't come with ia32 compatibility and are still kind
of successful.

On Mon, 26 May 2003, Mike A. Harris wrote:
>PPC is somewhat of an exception to the "everything must be x86
>compatible rule", because aside from x86, Macintosh is the second
>most commoditized consumer CPU.  PPC enjoys it's market segment
>by Macintosh legacy instead of x86 legacy, but the concept is the
>same

Apple successfully used an emulation technology to bridge the binary
incompatibility between 68k and PPC. Based on that precedent FX!32 would be
an equivalent route for Intel on Itanium.

What I find more interesting is that FX!32 software emulation is comparable
in performance (possibly superior?) than the Itanium native x86 microcode
done in Hardware.



