On Mon, 2003-05-26 at 08:38, Iain Barker wrote:
> What I find more interesting is that FX!32 software emulation is comparable
> in performance (possibly superior?) than the Itanium native x86 microcode
> done in Hardware.

It is superior, which makes for all the more irony.

IA-64's EPIC cannot issue parallel, microcoded x86 instructions.  So
it's ultra slow.

As such its performance, from what I understand, is only about one (1)
P4 MHz per three (3) IA-64 MHz for hardware x86 instructions.  So a
1.5GHz Intanium runs x86 at about the same speed as a 500MHz P4.

FX!32 (or whatever Intel is calling it now, I'm going to call it FX!32
;-), on the other hand, translates the x86 into native IA-64 after a few
runs.  So it actually becomes EPIC.  As such it's more like 1:1 P4 MHz
to IA-64.  So it's more like a 1.5GHz P4.


