On Mon, 2003-05-26 at 02:00, Mike A. Harris wrote:
> I disagree.  Linus is looking at it from a perspective of what
> processors are bound to be successful in volume based on the fact
> that some kind of x86 binary execution support with excellent
> performance is required in order to succeed in massive volume 
> sales.  You can design the best 32/64/128bit architecture since 
> sliced bread, make it totally legacy free with zero or close to 
> zero flaws - a computer of the Gods so to speak, and if it 
> doesn't have x86 execution capability with x86-like or better 
> performance, it wont ever be anything but a niche CPU used in 
> enterprise markets, and sell nowhere near the volume of the mass 
> market of x86 CPUs.

First off, I think you need to go back and read Linus' commentary.  He
was talking about design, independent of x86, as much as x86
compatibility.  While I don't totally disagree with his (and your)
commentary on x86 compatibility, I _do_ disagree with his commentary on
Alpha and why AMD x86-64 is better from an _architectural_ standpoint.

Secondly, if a processor, like Alpha, could offer unbelieveable
performance natively, and "as good" as performance for x86 (thanx to
FX!32), then it probably _would_have_ replaced the x86.  This was the
Alpha processor at the peak of 1997 -- operating at 500MHz when the best
Intel processor was the 200MHz Pentium Pro (and the 233-300MHz Pentium
II later that year).

1997 was a cross-roads, one where everything could have changed for the
better.  Even 64-bit NT/Alpha was available, and I ran FX!32 on a
production server NT server.  Alpha was good, but only lacked the
economies of scale Intel _could_ have brought to it.  But, alas, my
dreams were not to come to be.

Intel felt EPIC and Predication was the way to 64-bit.  Today they are
now fighting to "integrate" age-old run-time optimization and branch
predication "back in" to the IA-64 architecture.

> Granted, this depends on the ability of a manufacturer to be able
> to produce CPUs to meet demands, but that is a different
> discussion.  Assuming the capability to produce infinite CPUs,
> x86 compatibility is what will ultimately and undeniably
> determine if any CPU will ever reach the deployment size that x86
> currently holds.

While I do not disagree with you, Alpha with FX!32 offered "as good" as
performance for x86 using FX!32 and far better native performance -- in
1997.  Again, that was a year when Intel could have decided to "make the
switch."

> AMD has always been a good innovator IMHO, so I'm not too
> surprised personally.  I've had various AMD processors in the
> past, from AMD 386DX40 to various AMD 486 models,

At one point AMD fabricated many chips for Intel.  Oh how time has
changed with AMD barely able to afford one leading-edge fab.  This is
90% of their reasons for lagging Intel on packaging technology.

> K5, K6, K6-2, K6-3.

Ahhh, the NexGen Nx586 (second K5 release) and Nx686 (K6) cores.  Yes, I
loved NexGen and was happy when AMD scooped them up.

> I'd have several Athlon systems as well, if I didn't have
> so many Pentium family loaner systems. I can't justify buying 
> new hardware until these ones are too slow.  ;o)

I just upgraded from dual-Athlon to dual-Athlon MP recently, as I
figured I won't be upgrading to dual-Opteron until Q1 next year.

> Absolutely.  My real point however wasn't to show what 
> processor(s) are decent for doing certain computing tasks, or 
> which processor(s) are good for Linux developers, enthusiasts, 
> etc.  My point was to state that x86 legacy compatibility is the 
> absolute number one critical feature that any CPU requires in 
> order to be mass market commoditized.

And Alpha could have done it back in 1997.

Furthermore, some people forget that the first air-cooled, production
processor to 1GHz was a Samsung 21164A at 1.2GHz.

> That has nothing to do with Linux really, it has to do with what
> processors the mass market out there uses.  Right now that is x86,
> and the majority of them are running Windows.  In order to get another
> CPU out there that is just as popular as x86, it will have to do what
> x86 does now, and do it better.

Or be "as good" as x86, but do natively far much better, for little
additional cost.

Intel needs to do this with Itanium.  They have not -- not only from a
performance perspective, but the cost is still prohibitive.

Again, Alpha in 1997 could have done it.

> The mass market isn't ready to drop all of their existing binary
> applications and buy Alpha/PPC/MIPS/SPARC/whatever applications. 

We could have in 1997 with Alpha and FX!32.

> Nobody on the desktop (which is where the volume is) will purchase a
> CPU unless they can run their x86 software on it.

Again, we could have in 1997 with Alpha and FX!32.

> All other CPUs are for special and niche markets and will never 
> hit x86 volume levels.  AMD64 however is all that *and* a bucket 
> of chicken.

Yes, now that Alpha has been reduced to a trailing-edge product and
minimal production.

AMD x86-64 is all we have left to look forward to.

> This doesn't counter your statements at all however as you are 
> making one point which is not related to the point which I'm 
> making, and both are IMHO correct.

Thanx for agreed with me then.

> No, it has nothing to do with Windows vs. Linux vantage point.  
> I am not talking about what CPU is best for what OS, or for the
> users of that OS.

Huh?  One of the reasons I bought Alpha in 1997 was because Linux/Alpha
kicked the living crap out of Linux/x86.  I had _no_need_ for x86
compatibility on Linux -- and few _rarely_ do.

> I am talking about the more CPUs that go out the door, the
> cheaper those CPUs are, and the easier they are to get.  What
> currently sells the most number of CPUs, is what CPUs run 
> Microsoft Windows.  Any CPU that does not, will never reach the 
> volume of distribution that x86 does, and will thus be more 
> expensive because it doesn't sell in high volume.

Again, I think you are _incorrectly_ tying in x86 compatibility to
volume.  If a non-x86 product offers as-good of x86 execution speed but
far faster native speed, then it's a good candidate for replacing x86.

That's what Intel has tried (and utterly failed) to achive with IA-64. 
They will eventually do it -- but far, far later than Alpha could have. 
At least a decade I predict (2007 -- more like 2009).

> If someone is running Linux, and don't care what architecture you
> use as long as it does what you need, and don't care if you pay
> through the nose for a niche CPU, then the above probably doesn't
> matter.

Correct.

> Again, two different unrelated discussions however.

I disagree.

> Exactly.

Glad you agree with me.

> I'm running GinGin64 right now on an AMD64 processor, and just 
> about every bug I encounter has been fixed in rawhide already, so 
> before reporting bugs, check rawhide.  ;o)  I assume our x86_64 
> bits are hitting rawhide, but if they aren't, mention it and I'll 
> beat someone to a pulp internally.  ;o)

>From my understanding, Red Hat Enterprise Linux is still based on
"regular" Linux packages and Rawhide -- so the two are still of the same
"base" quality.  It's just the integration, QA and post-release support
that changes with Enterprise Linux (for obvious reasons).

> Red Hat either ships, has shipped, or will ship in the future,
> operating system products for a variety of CPU architectures
> including:
> x86, x86_64, alpha, ia64, ppc, ppc64, s390, s390x, sparc

PowerPC?  I guess I missed that one?

Is this related to IBM's rumors that it will produce Power4/5 and/or
PowerPC 970 mainboards for building commodity Linux systems?

> Alpha and sparc of course are no longer supported by Red Hat.  
> And since someone always asks...  Our ppc port is for IBM pSeries
> and iSeries high end server hardware, not for Macintosh.

Yes, that is what I assumed.

> Mac users who prefer Red Hat Linux operating systems, should use
> Yellow Dog Linux, it rocks.  <grin> Sparc users should use
> http://www.auroralinux.org - a port of RHL to sparc by other Red
> Hat employees (Tom Callaway, and others)

Right, I am familiar with it.  My current employer is a 100% Linux and
MacOSX shop -- I wield a PowerBook G4 myself.

> I will be getting a dual Opteron sometime before year's end I 
> think.  The most important thing for me performance wise is 
> compiler speed, and AMD64 builds XFree86 extremely fast.

Yep.  We were just discussing the performance of building a Gentoo
system over on our LUG.  One guy was "complaining" about the build time
on an Athlon XP2100+ -- until he tried it on a Pentium 4 2.66GHz.

64-bit ALUs should really speed up build times, as well as the other
fine-tuned enhancements AMD has made on the Athlon core in the x86-64.

> Yes, PAE is a horrible ugly hack.  ;o/

It's EMS all over again -- that's how I describe it to most people.


-- 
