On Mon, 2003-05-26 at 14:21, Bryan J. Smith wrote:
> Is this a joke?
> Or maybe I have been living a lie the last 5 years of my career?

Linux administrators are not really any less "inexpensive" than UNIX
administrators, developers, etc...  There is far greater of a gap
between Windows administrators/developers and Linux ones, than Linux and
UNIX.  As such, there is really no savings there.

It's all about cost of ownership.  Yeah, that buzzword everyone likes to
thrown around -- especially Microsoft.

Now Microsoft funded studies correctly show that in 4 out of 5
applications, Windows systems are cheaper to maintain.  Why?  Because
those same studies _assume_ your staff is 100% Windows, 0% Linux
knowledgeable, 100% entrenched in Windows and you'd have to pay for all
the re-training and conversion.  [ And even in the case of that 5th
application, web services, Linux was still cheaper in the long run --
funny. ]

Of course if you flip that study around and are talking about a _new_
firm, or a firm with _no_ investments in one area, then things are
radically different.  As a long time NT administrator/developer, I found
a single NT administrator could maintain 25-50 systems.  And as an
equally long UNIX/Linux administrator/developer, I found a single
UNIX/Linux administrator could maintain 150-200 systems.

Furthermore, as you move more into "development" where the company
"controls" its codebase, it's just the same.  With the Microsoft model,
I have to worry about what libraries and program versions I am _forced_
to support because Microsoft changes its products as it seems fit --
even if I'm only concerned with vertical/in-house applications.  With
the Linux model, *I* can control the _entire_ platform -- _regardless_
of what a distributor does to it.  That affects the bottom line.

UNIX has always given a lower cost of ownership from a support/
development standpoint.  But it did not offer commodity "initial cost"
like Windows did in both hardware and software, so it was often
discarded when it should not have been.  Linux basically came in and
broke that -- offering lower setup costs too, which defeated that common
Windows misnomer that many believe.

So don't break it down into simply being "cheap."  If being far more
maintainable with fewer support personnel is what you mean, then I
agree.


