On Mon, 2003-05-26 at 14:34, Bryan W. Headley wrote:
> No different than any other packager of GNU or Open Source software.

Er, no, I have to disagree with that statement.  Mandrake is an
excellent example of a distribution that is not -- read on.

Microsoft likes to play on this, and their point is not a bad one. 
Unfortunately for Microsoft, their statements are not factual in the
case of Red Hat, Debian and select others.

The key term here is "integrated" or "well tested."  We all know Open
Source software is well written, well tested and, in general, has many
other advantages because of its "peer review" approach.  But what
Microsoft likes to harp on is, again, "system integration testing."

We're talking about taking an entire distribution of packages, formally
testing them, having a form beta test, releasing them and doing
post-release support -- all following through with QA (Quality
Assurance) the rest of the way.  That is _exactly_ what Red Hat does,
and even Debian (in their "stable" releases).

I trust Red Hat's QA far more than any other commercial distribution --
from the Rawhide betas to the full distribution betas and post-release
updates.  The fact that they backport fixes to older versions and then
release those older versions as updates, instead of just releasing an
update with a newer version, goes to show the length they are willing to
go to ensure both fixes as well as compatibility and maintainability of
an existing system.

More surprisingly, Debian _also_ does this -- _unlike_ Mandrake and many
other distros out there.  They will maintain their "stable" tags in very
much the same was Red Hat does.  Unfortunately, Debian releases "stable"
versions much slower than Red Hat, with far older (but not any more
proven) packages than Red Hat.  Which is what keeps me from using Debian
on production workstations or servers -- although I still find it an
excellent distribution for development workstations.

> I believe I saw where Thailand was suing MS for the SQLServer 
> vulnerability. Ought to be interesting.

First off, I don't like the precendent that may be set by the case.  But
I'm a Libertarian and admit my views are not the most popular.

Secondly, I hope everyone here knows the _full_story_ on the SQL Slammer
worm.  The problem with SQL Slammer was _not_ that people were not
updating their systems.  No, the reason why SQL Slammer hit so bad was
because people _were_ updating their systems!  Yes, they were installing
patches from Microsoft that made their systems _vunerable_ to the SQL
Slammer worm after they patched!

You see, Microsoft didn't bother to test various and latter patches to
see if they "undid" the SQL patch released in October.  Many of them
did!  If you were lucky, Microsoft put a note in the release notes on
how some of these patches were "not compatible" with the SQL patch, and
the _manual_ way to re-integrate it.  Most sys admins didn't -- nor
should be have to bother.  Microsoft was extremely _negligable_ in their
actions.

Microsoft didn't integrate the SQL patch into a more "compatible" form
until Service Pack 3, which was released only a week before SQL Slammer
hit.  So even though most NT administors _do_ keep their systems updated
within 2-4 weeks of a patch, it didn't matter.  Microsoft did not care
to make sure different security patches and hot fixes did not conflict
with each other.

This is the general issue with Microsoft product design -- one that UNIX
does not have -- "High Integration."  High integration make be cool on
the desktop, and make administration somewhat easier on even the
server.  But it is the security nightmare.  And it will _continue_ to be
the security nightmare for Microsoft and those who support their
products for years to come as I detailed in this article:  

http://www.smithdot.net/display.php?category=freedom&article=msmonopolydoomed.txt#fudge3


I was working at a very large enterprise when the SQL Slammer hit (I
live in Orlando ;-).  It basically took us down for over 3 days.  We
were _very_ aggressive in patching our systems.  But _no_one_ in my
organization knew about the "little known fact" of why our SQL servers
were not patched -- to the point that several corporate IT managers were
in "hot water."  The truth was that we _were_ patched -- long ago.  But
then disabled by _automated_ Microsoft updates!

Again, so few people know this.  In fact, because Microsoft has _not_ be
"honest" regarding this whole issue, good and competent people have lost
their jobs!  If this is the type of "loyalty" Microsoft awards its
customers and IT professionals, then I might as well just "bend over"
and take it.  What a company!


