On Monday, May 26, 2003, at 04:16 AM, Stefan Reinauer wrote:
>> If there is no software for it.
>>
>> 32 bit X86 compatibility is the key.
>
> I don't think so. PPC and Power4 cpus don't come with ia32 
> compatibility
> and are still kind of successful.

No, the PPC is strictly in the Mac market, which is enough of a niche 
that folks are willing (still) to pay a premium price for the hardware 
because of the proprietary OS that runs on it. And, despite being only 
10% of the Intel market size, that is a still a large number.... many, 
many times larger than Alpha Marketing ever dreamed of being! (Yes, OS 
X is NOT Darwin. It is a superset of it.)

The Power4 is supported by IBM's customer base.

Now, come this summer with the Power960 (I think that's the number), 
that will change in that the need for a PowerPC chip will almost 
instantly vanish. ... and it may vanish instantly if IBM has the kinds 
of chip yield that Motorola has only dreamed about for the PowerPC.

Those of us in the Mac game assume that if IBM delivers on the 
"promise" of a 1 or 2 ghz 960, Apple will begin releasing hardware 
designed for it by MacWorld SF (January 2004), and rev the rest of 
their product line within 18 months. At that point, except for its 
existence in existing hardware, the PowerPC chip will be dead.


T.T.F.N.
