On Mon, 2003-05-26 at 18:23, William H. Magill wrote:
> Now, come this summer with the Power960 (I think that's the number), 
> that will change in that the need for a PowerPC chip will almost 
> instantly vanish. ... and it may vanish instantly if IBM has the kinds 
> of chip yield that Motorola has only dreamed about for the PowerPC.

The IBM PowerPC 970 is the first 64-bit IBM Power ISA product with
32-bit PowerPC ISA compatibility.

IBM's previous and only attempt was the defunct PowerPC 620 that never
left a brief sampling period years ago.  At was at that time when IBM
split the Power line into a new 64-bit variant without 32-bit PowerPC
compatibility.  That's also when Motorola began solely supplying all new
32-bit PowerPC designs to Apple.

> Those of us in the Mac game assume that if IBM delivers on the 
> "promise" of a 1 or 2 ghz 960,   Apple will begin releasing hardware 
> designed for it by MacWorld SF (January 2004), and rev the rest of 
> their product line within 18 months.

IBM's initial flagship PowerPC 970 will be released at 1.8GHz and will
consume 42W of power.  A lower speed 1.2GHz version will consume only
19W of power.  The latter is expected to be adopted in 64-bit Apple
notebooks.

Like the Power4, the PowerPC 970 can be used in a 16-node system
interconnect.  Unlike the Power4 the PowerPC 970 will not be available
in a dual-core version.  As such, the PowerPC 970 will only be available
in up to a 16-way, maximum on a single "fabric," whereas the Power4 is
available in up to a 32-way on a single "fabric."

> It that point, except for its existence in existing hardware,
> the PowerPC chip will be dead.

The IBM PowerPC 970 _is_ a PowerPC.  It is distinctly _separate_ from
their Power4 and IBM's planned Power5 series.  This is for several
reasons.

1)  PowerPC 970 = Economies of scale.  IBM wants a commodity Power
chip.  The PowerPC 970, and all future lineage, is that chip.  IBM plans
to sell them for both Apple systems as well as their own OEM and
in-house products.

2)  PowerPC 970 = Reduced fabrication quality.  IBM's Power chips are
designed with less than commodity materials and less-than "bleeding
edge" fabrication techniquies, improving longevity and reliability two
orders of magnitude over most ICs fabricated today.  This is not only
not necessary for commodity systems, but limits their performance.

3)  Power4/5/etc... = { 32-bit PowerPC }.  IBM will continue to develop
a "non-commodity, clean" 64-bit Power architecture that does not sport
32-bit PowerPC compatibility.  32-bit PowerPC ISA is not needed in any
of IBM's own platforms, only the Power ISA.

I'm sure there are other reasons too.


