In my tru64 programming days we used the 32 address space trick via -taso
and -xtaso_short.  The first flags the object as wanting only 32 bit
addresses and the second defaults the size of pointers used to 32bits.  All
of this made very forgiving and easy due to 64bit stack alignment and little
endianness of the alpha.  As I recall for times you needed it you could
actually in the program switch your pointer sizes via pragmas.  This was
most useful as the argv arguments to the program were 32 bit address space
64 bit sized pointers.  There were other similar needs too.  The Digital
compilers at the time came with documentation as to when you had to do
explicit pointer conversion and when you could let it automagically work.

